nce and that side branch  is relatively short this side branch is  a little bit longer it's just enough  longer but it's a little bit Wiggly and  when you pack all the broccoli together  all those side branches push against  each other and wiggle and look ugly  that's not okay because then when you  put them out on the grocery store shelf  they're not attractive enough and the  staff will toss them and this happens in  the springtime when it's they've been  growing under cold conditions and then  it starts to warm up and you start to  harvest them then that branch starts to  grow a little bit earlier than it would  otherwise place at a tiny detail but it  makes a difference between what you can  sell and not sell and there's another  difference that shows up if you look at  the open spaces in the top of this head  you'll see that they're a little bit  smaller than the open spaces in this one  and that means that you have to put four  or five more of these heads in a box  than you do of those and again they're  less firm  and so they don't ship as well so those  are issues that we still need to deal  with we're discovering this spring issue  is actually a fairly new one we didn't  know they were going to do that because  nobody was growing it in that season  before and that's we're working with  commercial breeders who have experience  in adjusting with all of these subtle  traits he's coming in because it looks  very normal at the end but behind it  we're doing we're really having a  revolution in the amount of consumption  where the production is happening how  we're getting the broccoli to market  from new areas and also the science  behind it learning a whole lot about how  flowers develop and so we're hoping to  see broccoli not develop additional  trait like presumptuousness  pretentiousness but it's continuing to  be one of the biggest comfort foods and  we want to make better broccoli locally  available for as much of the East Coast  as we can thank you this 